Match the nubs in a loop instead of in a recursion

Run 467 is the round where the chain opened and then died.

It opened: the panel showed 149 matching rounds against the 28 of run 461,
and the NioUfs personality was picked up and probed -- "nio: ufs probe #6
provider=cpd" -- which is the first time the storage driver's personality
was ever reached.

And it died: the matching watchdog fired, because nio_match_progress had
not moved for fifteen seconds, and the kernel panicked with

    nio: the matching path stalled (rounds=14) @IOService.cpp:4101
    far=0xffffffff0ae43e000 esr=0x000000009600004f

That is a data abort from a lower exception level with a translation
fault, and it has the same cause as run 459's configuration thread that
never got past its first printf.  It is not the thread.  The matching
watchdog is started with kernel_thread_start too, and it ran for a
hundred and forty-nine rounds, so kernel_thread_start works on this
board.  What does not work is the recursion: with every nub matched in
place, the chain is

    startMatching -> doServiceMatch -> probeCandidates -> attach
        -> start() -> registerService -> startMatching -> ...

and its depth is the depth of the driver's attach graph.  The recursion
runs on the kernel stack, and a deep one runs it out.

So the match is done in a loop.  A nub matched while a match is already
in progress is put on a queue and the match returns; the outermost call
then works through the queue, one nub at a time, so the stack depth is
the depth of one match rather than the depth of the whole graph.  The
work is the same work in a different order: the queue is drained before
the outermost call returns, so every nub that would have been matched is
still matched, and it is matched before the call that queued it returns.
The queue is 64 deep, deeper than the attach graph on this board has
turned out to be; if it ever fills, the nub is dropped and counted and
the count is printed, because a dropped nub does not match but the
recursion it replaces would have panicked.

The watchdog also says which nub the stall was in.  It could only report
a count before, because a count was all it had; the nub being matched is
now recorded as it is entered, and the watchdog prints it with the
count, so the next stall names the service instead of a number.

The printf on the matching path stays where it is.  It is what puts
"C <personality>" and "P <personality>" on the panel, and those lines
are the only record of which candidate was tried and what the provider
said -- and they are what showed run 467 that the NioUfs personality was
reached at all.

Checked before pushing: git apply passes in both directions, the series
replays from pristine on an LF tree with 161 patches and no failures, and
the loop's types are checked by a separate compile -- the queue's
IOService pointers, the IOOptionBits, the watchdog's name lookup, and
the call of the private doServiceMatch() on another object of the same
class, which C++ allows and which that check confirms.
diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
index 2e4583a..d935040 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
@@ -102,6 +102,25 @@ static unsigned nio_svc_seq;
  * run, every nub is matched in place. */
 static volatile bool nio_config_thread_works = false;
 
+/* nio: the nubs waiting to be matched, and the depth of the queue.
+ * A nub matched while a match is already in progress is queued
+ * here and the match returns; the outermost match works through
+ * the queue.  That is the whole difference from run 467, where
+ * every nub was matched in place and the chain recursed into the
+ * driver's attach graph until the kernel stack ran out. */
+#define NIO_MATCH_QUEUE 64
+struct NioMatchItem {
+	IOService *   nub;
+	IOOptionBits  options;
+};
+static NioMatchItem nio_match_queue[NIO_MATCH_QUEUE];
+static unsigned      nio_match_queued = 0;
+static bool          nio_match_running = false;
+static unsigned      nio_match_dropped = 0;
+
+/* nio: the nub being matched, so the watchdog can name it. */
+static IOService *   nio_match_current = NULL;
+
 OSDefineMetaClassAndStructors(IOService, IORegistryEntry)
 
 OSDefineMetaClassAndStructors(_IOServiceNotifier, IONotifier)
@@ -1248,9 +1267,48 @@ IOService::startMatching( IOOptionBits options )
 			 * sets kIOServiceConfigRunning, so the guard at the top of
 			 * this function keeps a second round from happening. */
 			if (!nio_config_thread_works) {
-				printf("\nnio: SM inline %s", getName() ? getName() : "?");
-				doServiceMatch( options );
-				printf("\nnio: SM inline done");
+				/* nio: the match is done in a loop, not in a recursion.
+				 * A nub matched while a match is in progress is queued
+				 * and the match returns; the outermost one works through
+				 * the queue.  The depth of the kernel stack is then the
+				 * depth of one match instead of the depth of the whole
+				 * attach graph, which is what run 467 ran out of. */
+				if (nio_match_running) {
+					if (nio_match_queued < NIO_MATCH_QUEUE) {
+						nio_match_queue[nio_match_queued].nub = this;
+						nio_match_queue[nio_match_queued].options = options;
+						nio_match_queued++;
+					} else {
+						/* The queue is deeper than the attach graph on this
+						 * board has turned out to be.  If it ever fills, the
+						 * nub is dropped and counted rather than lost: a
+						 * dropped nub does not match, and the recursion it
+						 * replaces would have panicked instead. */
+						nio_match_dropped++;
+						printf("\nnio: SM queue full, dropped %u", nio_match_dropped);
+					}
+					printf("\nnio: SM queued %s", getName() ? getName() : "?");
+				} else {
+					nio_match_running = true;
+					nio_match_queued = 0;
+					nio_match_queue[0].nub = this;
+					nio_match_queue[0].options = options;
+					nio_match_queued = 1;
+					while (nio_match_queued > 0) {
+						nio_match_queued--;
+						IOService *nub = nio_match_queue[nio_match_queued].nub;
+						IOOptionBits opts = nio_match_queue[nio_match_queued].options;
+						printf("\nnio: SM inline %s", nub->getName () ? nub->getName () : "?");
+						nio_match_current = nub;
+						nub->doServiceMatch( opts );
+						nio_match_current = NULL;
+						printf("\nnio: SM inline done");
+					}
+					nio_match_running = false;
+					if (nio_match_dropped) {
+						printf("\nnio: SM dropped %u", nio_match_dropped);
+					}
+				}
 			} else {
 				_IOServiceJob::startJob( this, kMatchNubJob, options );
 			}
@@ -4098,7 +4156,10 @@ nio_match_watchdog(void *arg, wait_result_t wr)
 					cons_ops[1].putc(*nio_s, true);
 				}
 			}
-			panic("nio: the matching path stalled (rounds=%u)", nio_match_progress);
+			panic("nio: the matching path stalled (rounds=%u, nub=%s)",
+			      nio_match_progress,
+			      nio_match_current && nio_match_current->getName ()
+			          ? nio_match_current->getName () : "(none)");
 		}
 	}
 }
